ies shared  my role in terms of that responsibility  at that time on the council there's then  been a period of time between that and  then I subsequently became the South  Shore police commissioner  with the benefit of Einstein and all the  lessons that have been learned with the  AMA fair select committee reports the  Children's Commissioners reports the  NSPCC and benardos there's been a huge  amount of learning and of course I've  taken all of this and made sure that  it's fully implemented in South  Yorkshire place and it's now making a  difference to many many young more young  people in South Yorkshire and in  Rotherham so I took my responsibilities  seriously I stood down in 2010 and I've  subsequently taken those lessons and  brought them to bear and South Yorkshire  Police even now what's your message for  the victims then today my message is  from my perspective clearly I'm very  sorry for any abuse that took place if I  could have prevented it I would any  right minded human being would want to  protect vulnerable children of that I am  convinced and all I can say is that this  is a top priority for South Yorkshire  Police it will remain a top priority for  South Yorkshire police for as long as  I'm in this role and I can only say that  I hope to see that service has been  improved more of this crime being  prevented more victims being protected  and more of the perpetrators being  prosecuted and of that I give an  absolute pledging commitment going  forward and briefly do you think you did  anything wrong  of course I shared I take my share of  the responsibility there was systemic  failure and I only wish that I knew more  at the time if I knew then what I know  now then clearly more could have been  done I think I took appropriate actions  where that information was available I  do have regrets that perhaps I weren't  more aware of the issue at the time  where I could have perhaps influenced  services better but in the end I regret  my role in that sy